     7	## Goal & constraints (USER-FACT)
     8	- the external party (untrusted, external) gets ONE code-server URL that can drive BOTH GPUs. All GPU
     9	  work goes through the queue (window-batch, prod-protected); no interactive card access.
    10	- Large model files (tens of GB) are warm-stored on the Synology (Vault-SCP, <lan-address>,
    11	  SSD-cached) and reused across jobs on both GPUs — not shipped per job.
    12	- The RTX sandbox (CT 205) is a NO-UPLINK L2 island; the GB10 sandbox is a docker
    13	  container on an `--internal` net. These isolation boundaries must NOT be weakened.
    14	
    15	## The design
    16	**One Synology NFS share is the shared substrate** (workspace + models + queue spool):
    17	```
    18	/volume1/the external party-warm/
    19	  workspace/            # the external party's scripts/configs (RW to the external party)
    20	  models/               # staged model files (RW to the external party, RO to jobs)
    21	  queue/rtx/{incoming,running,done}      # per-GPU spool
    22	  queue/gb10/{incoming,running,done}
    23	```
    24	Mounted via the HOST, then bind-mounted into each sandbox — so the island/internal
    25	sandboxes get the FILES without a network ROUTE to the NAS (isolation preserved):
    26	- **node2** mounts `the external party-warm` → bind into **CT 205** (RTX sandbox) at /workspace, /models(ro),
    27	  /queue; and into a NEW **CT 206 `the external party-code`** (unified code-server, node2, isolated,
    28	  bastion-reachable) RW.
    29	- **gb10** mounts `the external party-warm` → bind into the GB10 job/bench containers (same paths).
    30	- **node1** (arbiter host) mounts `the external party-warm` → arbiter reads/writes the spool LOCALLY
    31	  (replaces today's over-ssh spool reads).
    32	
    33	**Access:** retire the two per-GPU code-server endpoints; the bastion fronts ONE new
    34	ingress `the bench hostname` → CT 206 code-server, one CF Access app (OTP), policy allows
    35	jc@the external party.ai + korey@. `the external party-submit --host rtx|gb10` writes a spec into the matching
    36	`queue/<host>/incoming` on the share; the arbiter for that host drains it; jobs run in the
    37	GPU sandbox against the SAME NFS files the external party edited; results land in `queue/<host>/done`,
    38	visible instantly in his one workspace.
    39	
    40	## NFS specifics (proposed)
    41	- New export `the external party-warm`, **root_squash + all_squash to a dedicated `the external party` uid/gid**,
    42	  limited to the three host IPs (node1 .10, node2 .11, gb10 .225) — NOT the whole subnet,
    43	  NOT no_root_squash (unlike the existing proxmoxbackup exports).
    44	- Mount opts mirror the working GB10 precedent: `vers=3, soft, timeo=150, retrans=3,
    45	  _netdev, nofail, nolock`; models bind RO into sandboxes; workspace/queue RW.
    46	- uid alignment: Synology maps anon to uid 1025; the sandbox `the external party` users are 1000/1001.
    47	  The export will all_squash to one uid and the sandboxes' bind mounts run as that uid.
